Style Protocol Format Typical use REST HTTP/1.1 or HTTP/2 JSON / XML Public and internal web APIs — the dominant choice gRPC HTTP/2 Protocol Buffers (binary) High-performance internal service-to-service calls GraphQL HTTP JSON Flexible client-driven queries; Backend for Frontend (BFF) pattern WebSocket WebSocket protocol (starts as an HTTP <code>Upgrade</code>, then full-duplex over TCP) Any Real-time bidirectional communication — chat, live feeds, collaboration Message-based AMQP / Kafka protocol JSON / Avro Asynchronous event-driven integration between services <p>The same operation — "give me product 42" — looks quite different in each of the three request/response styles:</p> RESTgRPCGraphQL <pre><code>GET /products/42 HTTP/1.1
Accept: application/json
</code></pre> <p>The URL identifies the resource and the HTTP method says what to do. Any HTTP client (browser, <code>curl</code>, Postman) can call it.</p> <pre><code>service ProductService {
  rpc GetProduct (GetProductRequest) returns (Product);
}
message GetProductRequest { string id = 1; }
message Product { string id = 1; string name = 2; double price = 3; }
</code></pre> <p>The contract is a <code>.proto</code> file; code is generated for client and server, and messages travel as compact binary. Calling it needs a gRPC client — a browser cannot call it directly.</p> <pre><code>query {
  product(id: "42") { name price }
}
</code></pre> <p>A single endpoint (usually <code>POST /graphql</code>); the client chooses the fields it wants, which avoids over-fetching on mobile screens.</p> <p>How to choose. For the store's public API (browsers, mobile apps, partners), REST is the default: universal tooling, cacheable <code>GET</code>s, easy to debug. gRPC pays off for chatty internal calls where latency and payload size matter. GraphQL fits a front-end that composes data from many services. Messaging is the choice when the producer should not wait for the consumer — covered in Event-Driven Architecture.</p>

Small (1–5 devs)Medium (5–20 devs)Large (20+ devs) <p>Fewer than 10 services? Start here.</p> <pre><code>flowchart TD
    A{"Must it run on AWS?&lt;br/&gt;(credits, compliance)"}
    A --&gt;|No| B{"Users spread over&lt;br/&gt;several continents?"}
    B --&gt;|No| C["Railway / Render&lt;br/&gt;(git push → URL)"]
    B --&gt;|Yes| D["Fly.io&lt;br/&gt;(multi-region machines)"]
    A --&gt;|Yes| E{"One VM or a few&lt;br/&gt;containers is enough?"}
    E --&gt;|Yes| F["Lightsail&lt;br/&gt;(fixed monthly price)"]
    E --&gt;|No| G["ECS Express Mode&lt;br/&gt;(Fargate + ALB)"]</code></pre> <p>Key recommendation: Railway or Render for the fastest time-to-live — connect a GitHub repo and you have a live URL in minutes. On AWS, Lightsail when a fixed, predictable bill matters most; ECS Express Mode when you want a managed container platform that can later grow into full ECS without migrating.</p> <p>EKS at this scale</p> <p>The EKS control plane alone costs $0.10/hour ≈ $73/month<sup>1</sup>. Add a minimum node group (2 × <code>t3.medium</code> ≈ \(61/month), a load balancer and a database, and you are spending around **\)180/month** before any traffic — several times the whole bill on the simpler platforms (see section 5). For a small product team, Kubernetes is not an option here — it is a liability.</p> <p>10–50 services. You need production-grade infrastructure but probably not a full platform engineering team.</p> <pre><code>flowchart TD
    A{"Dedicated ops /&lt;br/&gt;platform engineer?"}
    B["ECS Fargate + ALB + RDS&lt;br/&gt;(serverless containers, no nodes)"]
    C["EKS (managed node groups)&lt;br/&gt;or ECS with more control"]

    A --&gt;|No| B
    A --&gt;|Yes| C</code></pre> <p>Why ECS Fargate for medium teams: no EC2 nodes to patch, and native integration with AWS IAM, Secrets Manager, ECR, and service discovery (Cloud Map / Service Connect). You lose the Kubernetes ecosystem but avoid the fixed control-plane fee and the node operations, which makes day-to-day work dramatically simpler.</p> <p>Fargate does not scale to zero by itself</p> <p>An ECS service keeps its <code>desiredCount</code> of tasks running and billing 24/7. Auto scaling adjusts that count between a minimum and a maximum; reaching zero requires setting the minimum (or a schedule) to 0 yourself, and the next request then has no task to serve it.</p> ECS Fargate EKS Node management None (serverless) You manage node groups (or pay for Auto Mode) Learning curve Low (task definitions) High (full K8s API) Ecosystem (Helm, CRDs) Limited Full Fixed cost before any workload None — pay per task ~$73/month control plane per cluster Multi-cloud portability AWS-only Portable (standard K8s API) GitOps (ArgoCD, Flux) Limited First-class <p>50+ services. EKS is the right answer — now the operational cost is justified.</p> <p>At this scale, you need:</p> <ul> <li>Managed node groups (or Karpenter for bin-packing autoscaling)</li> <li>ArgoCD for GitOps — declarative cluster state from Git</li> <li>AWS Load Balancer Controller — provision ALB/NLB from Kubernetes Ingress resources</li> <li>external-secrets-operator — sync Vault or AWS Secrets Manager secrets into Kubernetes Secrets</li> <li>Separate clusters per environment — dev, staging, prod each get their own EKS cluster</li> </ul> Add-on Purpose Karpenter Node autoscaling — provision exactly the right instance types for pending pods ArgoCD GitOps — declarative cluster state reconciled from Git AWS Load Balancer Controller Provision ALB/NLB from Kubernetes Ingress resources External Secrets Operator Sync Vault/Secrets Manager secrets into Kubernetes Secrets Metrics Server Enables HPA (Horizontal Pod Autoscaler) Cluster Autoscaler Alternative to Karpenter for node scaling <p>EKS Auto Mode</p> <p>Since December 2024, EKS Auto Mode lets AWS manage the worker nodes as well (Karpenter-based provisioning, OS patching) together with core add-ons such as load balancing and block storage, for a management fee on top of the EC2 price<sup>1</sup><sup>9</sup>. It reduces the node work in this list, not the need to understand Kubernetes.</p> <p>Then why does the course project use EKS?</p> <p>The project deploys the store on EKS with a team of 2–3 people — exactly what this class advises a startup not to do. The goal is different: the project exists to learn Kubernetes and AWS, not to minimise cost or time-to-market. Keep the cluster small, delete it when you are not using it, and use the Costs task to compare its bill with the alternatives from section 5.</p>

Algorithm How it works Bursts Memory per client Accuracy Typical use Token bucket Bucket of capacity <code>b</code> refilled at <code>r</code> tokens/s; each request takes a token Allows bursts up to <code>b</code> 2 numbers Exact API limits (AWS, Stripe-style) Leaky bucket Requests enter a FIFO drained at a constant rate Smooths them out (queues or drops) A queue or 2 numbers Exact Shaping traffic to a fragile backend Fixed window Counter per window <code>floor(now / w)</code> Up to 2× at window boundaries 1 number Weak at edges Coarse quotas (per day) Sliding window log Store every timestamp; count those in the last <code>w</code> None One entry per request Exact Low-volume, high-value limits Sliding window counter Current count + previous count × overlap fraction Small 2 numbers Approximate (assumes uniform previous window) Large-scale API limits (Cloudflare-style) GCRA Tracks a "theoretical arrival time"; equivalent to a token bucket Configurable 1 timestamp Exact Same as token bucket, less state <p>The boundary problem of fixed windows is easiest to see with a trace. Limit: 10 requests per second; a client sends 10 requests at t = 0.99 s and 10 more at t = 1.01 s:</p> ResultSource algorithm allowed of 20 <code>fixed_window</code> 20 <code>sliding_log</code> 10 <code>sliding_counter</code> 11 <code>token_bucket</code> 10 <pre><code># How many of 20 requests (10 at t=0.99 s, 10 at t=1.01 s) does each algorithm allow?
limit, window = 10, 1.0
trace = [0.99] * 10 + [1.01] * 10

def fixed_window(ts):
    counts, ok = {}, 0
    for t in ts:
        w = int(t // window)
        if counts.get(w, 0) &lt; limit:
            counts[w] = counts.get(w, 0) + 1
            ok += 1
    return ok

def sliding_log(ts):
    log, ok = [], 0
    for t in ts:
        log = [x for x in log if x &gt; t - window]
        if len(log) &lt; limit:
            log.append(t)
            ok += 1
    return ok

def sliding_counter(ts):
    counts, ok = {}, 0
    for t in ts:
        w = int(t // window)
        overlap = 1 - (t % window) / window
        estimate = counts.get(w - 1, 0) * overlap + counts.get(w, 0)
        if estimate &lt; limit:
            counts[w] = counts.get(w, 0) + 1
            ok += 1
    return ok

def token_bucket(ts, capacity=limit, rate=limit / window):
    tokens, last, ok = capacity, 0.0, 0
    for t in ts:
        tokens = min(capacity, tokens + (t - last) * rate)
        last = t
        if tokens &gt;= 1:
            tokens -= 1
            ok += 1
    return ok

print("| algorithm | allowed of 20 |\n|---|---:|")
for f in (fixed_window, sliding_log, sliding_counter, token_bucket):
    print(f"| `{f.__name__}` | {f(trace)} |")
</code></pre> <p>The fixed window lets 20 requests through in 20 ms — twice the limit — because they fall in two different windows. The log and the token bucket allow exactly 10. The sliding window counter allows 11: it assumes the previous window's requests were spread evenly, while here they all came at its very end — a small, bounded error in exchange for two numbers of state. This is why production limiters use a token bucket or a sliding window counter.</p>

The naive search<pre><code>SELECT id, title FROM events
WHERE title ILIKE '%tayler%' AND title ILIKE '%dezembro%';
</code></pre> <p>It scans the whole table (a leading <code>%</code> defeats B-tree indexes), finds nothing for the typo "tayler", does not know that "SP" means "São Paulo", ignores accents and plurals, and returns results in arbitrary order. Users expect typo tolerance, relevance ranking, prefix autocomplete and facets ("filter by city, date, genre") in tens of milliseconds.</p>

pgvector: vectors next to the data they describe<pre><code>CREATE EXTENSION IF NOT EXISTS vector;

CREATE TABLE kb_chunks (
    id        BIGSERIAL PRIMARY KEY,
    doc_id    TEXT NOT NULL,          -- 'refund-policy'
    content   TEXT NOT NULL,
    embedding vector(1024) NOT NULL
);

CREATE INDEX kb_chunks_embedding_idx
    ON kb_chunks USING hnsw (embedding vector_cosine_ops)
    WITH (m = 16, ef_construction = 64);

SET hnsw.ef_search = 40;              -- recall knob at query time
SELECT doc_id, content
FROM kb_chunks
ORDER BY embedding &lt;=&gt; :question_embedding   -- cosine distance
LIMIT 5;
</code></pre> Option Good when Watch out for pgvector in the existing PostgreSQL up to millions of vectors; you want filters, joins and transactions with the rest of the data index build time and memory on the primary; tune <code>ef_search</code> Search engine kNN (Elasticsearch, OpenSearch) you already run one; hybrid search (BM25 + vectors) in one query memory per shard Dedicated vector DB (Qdrant, Weaviate, Milvus, Pinecone) hundreds of millions of vectors, heavy filtering, multi-tenant one more system to run and keep in sync Redis / Valkey vector search small, hot sets with very low latency memory cost <p>Sizing, as a worked example: 100 k chunks × 1,024 dimensions × 4 bytes (float32) ≈ 400 MB of raw vectors, plus the HNSW graph (with <code>m = 16</code>, roughly a few hundred bytes per vector) — it fits comfortably in the catalog database. At 100 M vectors (≈ 400 GB raw) quantization or a dedicated cluster becomes worth discussing.</p>

Spring Cloud GatewayNginxManaged <p>Spring Cloud Gateway ships a Redis-backed token bucket (<code>RequestRateLimiter</code>), used in hands-on 4:</p> application.yaml<pre><code>spring:
  cloud:
    gateway:
      server:
        webflux:      # the property prefix since Spring Cloud 2025 (as in hands-on 4)
          routes:
            - id: inventory-holds
              uri: http://inventory:8080
              predicates:
                - Path=/api/holds/**
              filters:
                - name: RequestRateLimiter
                  args:
                    redis-rate-limiter.replenishRate: 10   # tokens per second
                    redis-rate-limiter.burstCapacity: 20   # bucket capacity
                    redis-rate-limiter.requestedTokens: 1  # cost of one request
                    key-resolver: "#{@userKeyResolver}"
</code></pre> RateLimitConfig.java<pre><code>@Configuration
public class RateLimitConfig {

    @Bean
    KeyResolver userKeyResolver() {   // the key: the authenticated user, or the IP
        return exchange -&gt; exchange.getPrincipal()
            .map(Principal::getName)
            .switchIfEmpty(Mono.fromSupplier(() -&gt;
                exchange.getRequest().getRemoteAddress().getAddress().getHostAddress()));
    }
}
</code></pre> <p>Nginx limits per key with a leaky bucket, used in hands-on 3:</p> nginx.conf<pre><code># 10 MB of shared memory holds about 160 000 IP states
limit_req_zone $binary_remote_addr zone=per_ip:10m rate=10r/s;

server {
    location /api/ {
        limit_req zone=per_ip burst=20 nodelay;   # absorb bursts of 20, no queueing delay
        limit_req_status 429;
        proxy_pass http://gateway;
    }
}
</code></pre> <ul> <li>AWS API Gateway: usage plans with throttling (rate + burst, a token bucket) and quotas per API key; AWS WAF rate-based rules per IP.</li> <li>GCP: Apigee quotas and spike arrest; Cloud Armor rate-limiting rules.</li> <li>Azure API Management: <code>rate-limit-by-key</code> and <code>quota-by-key</code> policies.</li> <li>Cloudflare: rate-limiting rules at the edge (sliding window).</li> </ul> <p>Try it. Load the rate-limiter scenario and run the spike profile without limits: the backend saturates and p99 explodes for everyone. Turn on the gateway rate limit and tune its rate and burst: watch the 429s absorb the excess while p99 for accepted requests stays flat. What happens if the limit is set higher than the backend's capacity?</p> <p> Open the full playground</p> <p>The full design of a distributed rate limiter, as an interview problem, is in the rate limiter case study.</p>

Step Service Local transaction Compensation Kind 1 <code>order</code> Create order <code>PENDING</code> Mark order <code>CANCELLED</code> compensatable 2 <code>inventory</code> Hold seats B-12, B-13 (hold expires in 10 min) Release the seats compensatable 3 <code>payment</code> Charge the card through the provider (idempotency key = order id) Refund pivot 4 <code>inventory</code> Mark seats <code>SOLD</code> — retriable 5 <code>order</code> Mark order <code>CONFIRMED</code>, publish <code>OrderConfirmed</code> — retriable <p>The pivot is the go/no-go point. Before it, every step can be compensated; after it, the saga never goes back — the remaining steps are retriable: they must eventually succeed, retried as many times as needed. That is why the seat hold must outlive the payment: if the hold could expire while the card is being charged, step 4 could fail permanently after the pivot and force a refund — a bad experience the design should make impossible (or at least rare and monitored).</p> <p>There are two ways to coordinate the steps.</p> ChoreographyOrchestration <p>No central coordinator: each service reacts to the others' events.</p> <pre><code>sequenceDiagram
    participant O as order
    participant I as inventory
    participant P as payment
    O-&gt;&gt;I: OrderPlaced
    I-&gt;&gt;I: hold seats
    I-&gt;&gt;P: SeatsHeld
    P-&gt;&gt;P: charge card
    alt payment succeeded
        P-&gt;&gt;I: PaymentCaptured
        I-&gt;&gt;I: mark seats SOLD
        I-&gt;&gt;O: SeatsSold
        O-&gt;&gt;O: CONFIRMED, publish OrderConfirmed
    else payment failed
        P-&gt;&gt;I: PaymentFailed
        I-&gt;&gt;I: release seats (compensation)
        I-&gt;&gt;O: SeatsReleased
        O-&gt;&gt;O: CANCELLED (compensation)
    end</code></pre> <p>A saga orchestrator (here, inside <code>order</code>) sends commands and reacts to replies. The whole flow is in one place.</p> <pre><code>sequenceDiagram
    participant S as purchase saga (order)
    participant I as inventory
    participant P as payment
    S-&gt;&gt;I: HoldSeats
    I--&gt;&gt;S: SeatsHeld
    S-&gt;&gt;P: ChargeCard (key o-7731)
    alt charged
        P--&gt;&gt;S: PaymentCaptured
        S-&gt;&gt;I: MarkSeatsSold
        I--&gt;&gt;S: SeatsSold
        S-&gt;&gt;S: CONFIRMED, publish OrderConfirmed
    else declined
        P--&gt;&gt;S: PaymentFailed
        S-&gt;&gt;I: ReleaseSeats (compensation)
        I--&gt;&gt;S: SeatsReleased
        S-&gt;&gt;S: CANCELLED
    end</code></pre> <p>The order's status is the saga's state, so the saga is a state machine:</p> <pre><code>stateDiagram-v2
    [*] --&gt; PENDING
    PENDING --&gt; SEATS_HELD : SeatsHeld
    PENDING --&gt; CANCELLED : NoSeats
    SEATS_HELD --&gt; PAID : PaymentCaptured (pivot)
    SEATS_HELD --&gt; RELEASING : PaymentFailed / timeout
    RELEASING --&gt; CANCELLED : SeatsReleased
    PAID --&gt; CONFIRMED : SeatsSold (retried until success)
    CONFIRMED --&gt; [*]
    CANCELLED --&gt; [*]</code></pre> <p>A minimal orchestrator handles each reply in a local transaction, moves the state machine and sends the next command through the outbox:</p> <pre><code>@Component
@RequiredArgsConstructor
public class PurchaseSaga {

    private final SagaRepository sagas;
    private final CommandPublisher commands;          // writes commands to the outbox

    @Transactional
    public void handle(SagaReply reply) {
        PurchaseState s = sagas.lock(reply.orderId());  // SELECT ... FOR UPDATE
        if (s.alreadyApplied(reply.replyId())) return;  // duplicate reply: ignore
        switch (s.step()) {
            case HOLDING_SEATS -&gt; {
                if (reply.ok()) { s.moveTo(CHARGING);  commands.send(new ChargeCard(s.orderId(), s.total())); }
                else            { s.moveTo(CANCELLED); commands.send(new CancelOrder(s.orderId())); }
            }
            case CHARGING -&gt; {
                if (reply.ok()) { s.moveTo(CONFIRMING); commands.send(new MarkSeatsSold(s.orderId())); }
                else            { s.moveTo(RELEASING);  commands.send(new ReleaseSeats(s.orderId())); }
            }
            case CONFIRMING -&gt; { s.moveTo(CONFIRMED); commands.send(new ConfirmOrder(s.orderId())); }
            case RELEASING  -&gt; { s.moveTo(CANCELLED); commands.send(new CancelOrder(s.orderId())); }
            default -&gt; throw new IllegalStateException("unexpected reply in " + s.step());
        }
        sagas.save(s.applied(reply.replyId()));
    }
}
</code></pre> Choreography Orchestration Coordination Each service reacts to events One orchestrator sends commands and tracks state Coupling Services know each other's events Participants only know their commands; the orchestrator knows the flow Visibility of the flow Spread over several codebases; hard to answer "where is order o-7731?" One state machine; easy to inspect, test and change Risk Cyclic dependencies, "event spaghetti" as steps grow The orchestrator can become a god service holding business logic Good for 2–3 steps, simple flows 4+ steps, timeouts, branches — like the purchase <p>In production, orchestrated sagas are often written on a durable execution / workflow engine — Temporal, AWS Step Functions, Azure Durable Functions, Camunda — which persists the state after each step, handles timers ("release the hold if no payment in 10 minutes") and retries, and survives restarts of the process running it.</p>

order/src/main/java/boxoffice/order/OrderService.java<pre><code>package boxoffice.order;

import com.fasterxml.jackson.databind.ObjectMapper;
import java.time.Instant;
import java.util.Map;
import java.util.UUID;
import org.springframework.jdbc.core.JdbcTemplate;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import org.springframework.transaction.annotation.Transactional;

@Service
public class OrderService {

    private final JdbcTemplate jdbc;
    private final ObjectMapper json;

    public OrderService(JdbcTemplate jdbc, ObjectMapper json) {
        this.jdbc = jdbc;
        this.json = json;
    }

    @Transactional
    public UUID confirm(NewOrder cmd) throws Exception {
        UUID orderId = UUID.randomUUID();
        Instant now = Instant.now();
        jdbc.update("""
            INSERT INTO orders (id, event_id, seats, customer_email, status, confirmed_at)
            VALUES (?, ?, ?, ?, 'CONFIRMED', ?)
            """, orderId, cmd.eventId(), cmd.seats(), cmd.customerEmail(), java.sql.Timestamp.from(now));

        UUID eventId = UUID.randomUUID();
        Map&lt;String, Object&gt; envelope = Map.of(
            "eventId", eventId.toString(),
            "type", "OrderConfirmed",
            "version", 1,
            "occurredAt", now.toString(),
            "orderId", orderId.toString(),
            "customerEmail", cmd.customerEmail(),
            "seats", cmd.seats());
        jdbc.update("""
            INSERT INTO outbox (id, aggregate_type, aggregate_id, event_type, payload)
            VALUES (?, 'order', ?, 'OrderConfirmed', ?::jsonb)
            """, eventId, orderId.toString(), json.writeValueAsString(envelope));
        return orderId; // both rows commit together, or neither does
    }
}
</code></pre> <p>In this exercise an order is confirmed when it is created (the payment is out of scope); if your service has a separate confirm step, write the outbox row there.</p>

notification/src/main/java/boxoffice/notification/TicketEmailService.java<pre><code>package boxoffice.notification;

import com.fasterxml.jackson.databind.JsonNode;
import com.fasterxml.jackson.databind.ObjectMapper;
import java.util.UUID;
import org.springframework.jdbc.core.JdbcTemplate;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import org.springframework.transaction.annotation.Transactional;

@Service
public class TicketEmailService {

    private final JdbcTemplate jdbc;
    private final ObjectMapper json;

    public TicketEmailService(JdbcTemplate jdbc, ObjectMapper json) {
        this.jdbc = jdbc;
        this.json = json;
    }

    @Transactional
    public void handle(String message) {
        JsonNode event = parse(message);
        UUID eventId = UUID.fromString(event.path("eventId").asText());
        int inserted = jdbc.update(
            "INSERT INTO processed_events (event_id) VALUES (?) ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING", eventId);
        if (inserted == 0) {
            return; // duplicate delivery: already processed, skip the side effect
        }
        jdbc.update("INSERT INTO ticket_emails (order_id, event_id, recipient) VALUES (?, ?, ?)",
            UUID.fromString(event.path("orderId").asText()), eventId, event.path("customerEmail").asText());
        // The real e-mail is sent from ticket_emails by a separate sender,
        // using event_id as the idempotency key with the e-mail provider.
    }

    private JsonNode parse(String message) {
        try {
            JsonNode event = json.readTree(message);
            if (!"OrderConfirmed".equals(event.path("type").asText()) || event.path("eventId").isMissingNode()) {
                throw new InvalidEventException("unexpected event: " + message);
            }
            return event;
        } catch (InvalidEventException e) {
            throw e;
        } catch (Exception e) {
            throw new InvalidEventException("malformed event: " + e.getMessage());
        }
    }
}
</code></pre> notification/src/main/java/boxoffice/notification/KafkaConfig.java<pre><code>package boxoffice.notification;

import org.apache.kafka.clients.admin.NewTopic;
import org.apache.kafka.clients.consumer.ConsumerRecord;
import org.apache.kafka.common.TopicPartition;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;
import org.springframework.kafka.annotation.KafkaListener;
import org.springframework.kafka.config.TopicBuilder;
import org.springframework.kafka.core.KafkaTemplate;
import org.springframework.kafka.listener.DeadLetterPublishingRecoverer;
import org.springframework.kafka.listener.DefaultErrorHandler;
import org.springframework.kafka.support.ExponentialBackOffWithMaxRetries;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;

@Configuration
public class KafkaConfig {

    @Bean
    NewTopic orderEvents() {
        return TopicBuilder.name("order-events").partitions(3).replicas(1).build();
    }

    @Bean
    NewTopic orderEventsDlt() {
        return TopicBuilder.name("order-events.DLT").partitions(3).replicas(1).build();
    }

    // Picked up by Spring Boot's listener container factory.
    @Bean
    DefaultErrorHandler errorHandler(KafkaTemplate&lt;String, String&gt; template) {
        DeadLetterPublishingRecoverer recoverer = new DeadLetterPublishingRecoverer(template,
            (record, ex) -&gt; new TopicPartition(record.topic() + ".DLT", record.partition()));
        ExponentialBackOffWithMaxRetries backOff = new ExponentialBackOffWithMaxRetries(3);
        backOff.setInitialInterval(500);
        backOff.setMultiplier(2.0);
        DefaultErrorHandler handler = new DefaultErrorHandler(recoverer, backOff);
        handler.addNotRetryableExceptions(InvalidEventException.class); // poison: straight to the DLT
        return handler;
    }

    @Component
    static class OrderEventsListener {
        private final TicketEmailService service;

        OrderEventsListener(TicketEmailService service) {
            this.service = service;
        }

        @KafkaListener(topics = "order-events", groupId = "notification")
        void onMessage(ConsumerRecord&lt;String, String&gt; record) {
            service.handle(record.value());
        }
    }
}
</code></pre> <p><code>InvalidEventException</code> is a plain <code>RuntimeException</code>. The consumer commits the offset only after <code>handle</code> returns (Spring Kafka's default <code>BATCH</code>/<code>RECORD</code> ack modes commit after the listener)<sup>2</sup>, so a crash in the middle means redelivery — which the <code>processed_events</code> table absorbs.</p>

load/orders.js<pre><code>import http from 'k6/http';
import { check } from 'k6';

const BASE = __ENV.BASE_URL || 'http://localhost:8081';

export const options = {
  scenarios: {
    orders: {
      executor: 'constant-arrival-rate',
      rate: Number(__ENV.RATE || 20), timeUnit: '1s',
      duration: __ENV.DURATION || '10m',
      preAllocatedVUs: 20, maxVUs: 200,
    },
  },
};

export default function () {
  const body = JSON.stringify({
    eventId: 1 + Math.floor(Math.random() * 100),
    seats: 1 + Math.floor(Math.random() * 4),
    customerEmail: `fan${__VU}-${__ITER}@example.com`,
  });
  const res = http.post(`${BASE}/orders`, body, { headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' } });
  check(res, { 'order accepted': (r) =&gt; r.status === 201 });
}
</code></pre> chaos/failures.sh<pre><code>#!/usr/bin/env bash
# Failure schedule, started together with the 10-minute k6 run.
set -x
sleep 90;  docker compose kill order;          sleep 20; docker compose start order
sleep 90;  docker compose stop kafka;          sleep 60; docker compose start kafka
sleep 90;  docker compose kill notification;   sleep 20; docker compose start notification
sleep 30
# a duplicate: publish again an event that was already published
