licts and retries, while pessimistic locking serializes the requests neatly. Measure your contention before choosing — and remember that the real flash-sale design also reduces contention before the database: a waiting room (lab 7) admits fans at a rate the inventory can absorb.</p> <p>In the interview</p> <p>For "design Ticketmaster", say explicitly: "seat inventory is the one place that needs strong consistency; I will use a relational database with row-level locking (or conditional updates) for holds, with a TTL on each hold, and everything else — catalog, search, seat maps — can be eventually consistent and cached." Then mention the hot-row problem: thousands of fans on the same 100 seats serialize on their locks, so queue them before the database.</p> <p>Next: 2. Replica and pooling.</p>

services/inventory/src/main/java/boxoffice/inventory/DataSourceConfig.java<pre><code>package boxoffice.inventory;

import com.zaxxer.hikari.HikariDataSource;
import javax.sql.DataSource;
import org.springframework.boot.context.properties.ConfigurationProperties;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Primary;
import org.springframework.jdbc.datasource.LazyConnectionDataSourceProxy;

/**
 * Two connection pools — primary and replica — behind one DataSource.
 * A transaction marked @Transactional(readOnly = true) gets a replica connection;
 * every other transaction gets a primary connection. The choice happens when the
 * transaction runs its first statement (that is what "lazy" means here).
 */
@Configuration
public class DataSourceConfig {

    @Bean
    @ConfigurationProperties("inventory.datasource.primary")
    HikariDataSource primaryDataSource() {
        return new HikariDataSource();
    }

    @Bean
    @ConfigurationProperties("inventory.datasource.replica")
    HikariDataSource replicaDataSource() {
        return new HikariDataSource();
    }

    @Bean
    @Primary
    DataSource dataSource(HikariDataSource primaryDataSource, HikariDataSource replicaDataSource) {
        var routing = new LazyConnectionDataSourceProxy(primaryDataSource);
        routing.setReadOnlyDataSource(replicaDataSource);     // Spring Framework 6.2+
        return routing;
    }
}
</code></pre> <p><code>LazyConnectionDataSourceProxy</code> hands out a placeholder connection when a transaction starts and fetches the real one at the first statement — by then Spring knows whether the transaction is <code>readOnly</code>, and picks the pool. The rule becomes an annotation:</p> Method Annotation Goes to Why <code>SeatQueries.availability</code>, <code>seats</code> <code>@Transactional(readOnly = true)</code> replica browsing; a few ms of staleness is fine <code>HoldService.hold</code>, <code>confirm</code>, <code>release</code> <code>@Transactional</code> primary writes <code>HoldService.find</code> <code>@Transactional</code> primary read-your-writes: the fan just created this hold <code>SeatQueries.doubleBookedSeats</code> <code>@Transactional</code> primary an audit must not be stale <p>Both pools publish metrics (<code>hikaricp.connections.active{pool=primary|replica}</code>): check them while a test runs, <code>curl -s 'localhost:8083/actuator/metrics/hikaricp.connections.active?tag=pool:replica' | jq</code>.</p>

