services/notification/src/main/java/boxoffice/notification/NotificationService.java<pre><code>package boxoffice.notification;

import java.time.OffsetDateTime;
import java.time.ZoneOffset;
import org.slf4j.Logger;
import org.slf4j.LoggerFactory;
import org.springframework.jdbc.core.simple.JdbcClient;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import org.springframework.transaction.annotation.Transactional;

@Service
public class NotificationService {

    private static final Logger log = LoggerFactory.getLogger(NotificationService.class);

    private final JdbcClient jdbc;
    private final EmailSender email;
    private final NotificationProperties props;

    public NotificationService(JdbcClient jdbc, EmailSender email, NotificationProperties props) {
        this.jdbc = jdbc;
        this.email = email;
        this.props = props;
    }

    /** @return true if an e-mail was sent, false if the event was a duplicate */
    @Transactional
    public boolean handle(OrderEvent e) {
        if (props.idempotent()) {
            int fresh = jdbc.sql("INSERT INTO processed_events (event_id) VALUES (?) ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING")
                    .param(e.eventId())
                    .update();
            if (fresh == 0) {
                log.info("duplicate event {} for order {} ignored", e.eventId(), e.orderId());
                return false;
            }
        }
        email.orderReceived(e); // throws -&gt; the whole transaction rolls back, the dedupe row too
        jdbc.sql("""
                INSERT INTO notifications (event_id, order_id, email, kind, occurred_at)
                VALUES (?, ?, ?, ?, ?)
                """)
                .params(e.eventId(), e.orderId(), e.customerEmail(), e.eventType(),
                        OffsetDateTime.ofInstant(e.occurredAt(), ZoneOffset.UTC))
                .update();
        return true;
    }
}
</code></pre> <p>The order of operations is the whole point:</p> <ol> <li>Claim the event: <code>INSERT ... ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING</code> on the primary key <code>event_id</code>. Zero rows    means another delivery (or another consumer instance, concurrently) already claimed it → skip.</li> <li>Do the effect — send the e-mail.</li> <li>Commit. If step 2 throws, the claim is rolled back and the retry can try again.</li> </ol> <p>One window remains: the e-mail is sent and then the commit fails (database down at that exact instant). The redelivery will send a second e-mail. No consumer can make an external side effect exactly-once on its own; the complete fix is an idempotency key accepted by the provider (most e-mail and payment APIs take one — pass the <code>eventId</code>). Exactly-once processing inside Kafka (transactions, <code>read_committed</code>) covers Kafka-to-Kafka pipelines, not e-mails.</p> <p>In the interview</p> <p>"Kafka gives at-least-once; I make consumers idempotent with a dedupe table keyed by event id, written in the same transaction as the effect, and pass the event id as the idempotency key to external providers." That sentence answers most "what about duplicates?" follow-ups.</p>

